evant information visible. The text is legible, and there are no missing or unclear elements.\n\nStep 2: User located and lock confirmed\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → Users → Search bar\nSearch by employee ID. Confirm the account status badge shows Locked before continuing — do not reset a password on an account that isn't actually locked without further checks.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 2 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.90 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, with a user's information displayed. The user's name is SOL-04821, and their role is J. Employee.\n\n*   **CoreID Admin Console**\n    *   The CoreID Admin Console is a web-based interface for managing users, tickets, and settings.\n    *   It has a dark blue color scheme with white text and icons.\n    *   The console is divided into several sections, including the navigation menu, dashboard, and user information.\n*   **User Information**\n    *   The user's name is SOL-04821.\n    *   Their role is J. Employee.\n    *   The user's ID is SOL-04821.\n    *   The user's email address is not visible in the screenshot.\n*   **Navigation Menu**\n    *   The navigation menu is located on the left side of the screen.\n    *   It has several options, including \"Admin Console\", \"Dashboard\", \"Tickets\", and \"Settings\".\n    *   The menu is collapsible, allowing users to hide or show the options.\n*   **Dashboard**\n    *   The dashboard is the main section of the console.\n    *   It displays a list of users, tickets, and settings.\n    *   The dashboard is customizable, allowing users to add or remove widgets.\n*   **Tickets**\n    *   The tickets section is located below the dashboard.\n    *   It displays a list of open and closed tickets.\n    *   Users can create new tickets, view ticket details, and update ticket status.\n*   **Settings**\n    *   The settings section is located at the bottom of the console.\n    *   It allows users to configure various settings, such as user roles and permissions.\n    *   Users can also view system logs and error messages in this section.\n\nIn summary, the CoreID Admin Console is a web-based interface for managing users, tickets, and settings. The console has a dark blue color scheme with white text and icons, and is divided into several sections, including the navigation menu, dashboard, and user information. Users can create new tickets, view ticket details, and update ticket status in the tickets section. They can also configure various settings, such as user roles and permissions, in the settings section. Overall, the console provides a user-friendly interface for managing various aspects of the system.\n\nStep 3: Caller identity verified\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → user profile → Identity Verification panel\nAsk the caller for at least 2 of: employee ID, manager's name, department, or last 4 digits of the phone on file. ✅ Do not send a one-time code to the user — your own admin session already satisfies the authentication requirement; this checklist is what verifies the caller is who they claim to be. If fewer than 2 items match, stop and escalate to your team lead rather than proceeding.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 3 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.90 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of a CoreID Admin Console page, with a white background and a dark blue sidebar on the left. The page is titled \"J. Employee - Identity Verification\" and has a list of options below it.\n\n*   **Title and Options**\n    *   The title is \"J. Employee - Identity Verification\".\n    *   There are two options: \"Employee ID matches ticket\" and \"Manager name matches ticket\".\n    *   Both options have a checkbox next to them.\n*   **Checkbox Status**\n    *   The first option, \"Employee ID matches ticket\", has a checked box.\n    *   The second option, \"Manager name matches ticket\", has an unchecked box.\n*   **Additional Information**\n    *   Below the options, there is a message that says \"2 of 4 confirmed - minimum required to proceed\".\n*   **Sidebar**\n    *   The sidebar has a list of links, including \"Admin Console\", \"Dashboard\", \"Tickets\", \"Users\", \"Reports\", and \"Settings\".\n\nOverall, the image appears to be a screenshot of a CoreID Admin Console page, showing a verification process for an employee's identity. The page has a simple design with a white background and a dark blue sidebar, and it includes a list of options and checkboxes for verifying employee information.\n\nStep 4: Temporary password issued\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → user profile → Reset Password\nSelect Generate Temporary Password. Leave Force change at next sign-in checked — this is the default and it must not be disabled.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 4 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.95 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of a password reset page for an employee named J. Employee, with a temporary password provided and a button to generate a new one.\n\n* **CoreID Admin Console**\n\t+ The top-left corner of the image has a dark blue bar with white text that reads \"CoreID\" and \"Admin Console\".\n* **Reset Password - J. Employee**\n\t+ The main content area of the page is a light gray box with black text that reads \"Reset Password - J. Employee\".\n\t+ Below this, there is a temporary password field with the text \"Tq7#mVr2lklp9x\" and a checkbox next to it that says \"Force change at next sign-in\".\n\t+ There is a blue button below the checkbox that says \"Generate Temporary Password\".\n* **User Menu**\n\t+ On the left side of the image, there is a dark blue sidebar with white text that lists various options, including \"Users\", \"Reports\", \"Settings\", and \"Dashboard\".\n* **Signed in as**\n\t+ At the top of the image, there is a light gray bar with white text that reads \"Signed in as: A. Rao (L1 Support - Password Reset Admin)\".\n\n**Summary*