See [CISA's guidance on requiring strong passwords](https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world/require-strong-passwords) for the reasoning behind credential-handling policy.\nStep 6: Ticket resolved with audit note\nNavigation: Ticketing system → ticket → Resolution notes\nLog what was done (identity checks matched, temporary password issued, force-change enabled) and set the ticket to Resolved. This note is what satisfies the audit trail requirement — an admin-mediated reset with no note is a compliance gap.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 5 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.95 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, with a ticket resolution page open. The page displays the ticket number, status, and resolution notes.\n\n*   **Ticket #SOL-84213 - Resolution**\n    *   Status: Resolved\n    *   Resolution notes: Identity verified (2 of 4 checks). Temporary password issued via CoreID. Force-change-at-next-sign-in enabled. No OTP sent - admin-mediated reset per policy.\n*   **CoreID Admin Console**\n    *   Dashboard\n    *   Tickets\n    *   Users\n    *   Reports\n    *   Settings\n\nThe image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, with a ticket resolution page open. The page displays the ticket number, status, and resolution notes. The ticket is resolved, and the resolution notes indicate that the identity was verified, a temporary password was issued, and the force-change-at-next-sign-in feature was enabled. There are no other visual elements or text in the image. \n\n**Summary:** The image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, with a ticket resolution page open. The ticket is resolved, and the resolution notes indicate that the identity was verified, a temporary password was issued, and the force-change-at-next-sign-in feature was enabled.\n\n**Detailed Description:**\n\n*   The image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console.\n*   The page displays the ticket number, status, and resolution no